Cudy TR3600 v1: fix the PWM fan channel and the thermal ramp (Kwrt)

Apply AFTER the Kwrt patch series (file name sorts last, "zz-").

Kwrt's tr3600.patch wires the pwm-fan to PWM channel 1 and selects the
pwm1_pins group (groups = "pwm1_0"), which on MT7987 is PWM1@PIN7 - a pin
shared with JTAG.  On the real board the fan sits on PWM0@PIN13, driven by
the pwm_pins group that mt7987.dtsi already defines.

With the wrong channel the driver binds, the cooling device appears and
cur_state still changes, so nothing looks broken - but PWM0 is never
claimed, sits at its idle level and the fan runs at full speed with no
control at all.  Verified on hardware: /sys/kernel/debug/pwm must list
"pwm-0 (pwm-fan)"; with the Kwrt binding it lists "pwm-1 (pwm-fan)" and
pwm-0 is owned by sysfs only.

Second fix: mt7987.dtsi maps the 40 C trip to fan cooling level 1, so the
fan runs at 50 % whenever the SoC is above 40 C - i.e. always.  The ramp is
moved to 68/78/88 C with 5 C hysteresis and cooling-levels
<0 128 200 255> (off / 50 % / 78 % / 100 %).

Signed-off-by: TR3600-Kwrt <noreply@example.com>
---
diff --git a/target/linux/mediatek/dts/mt7987b-cudy-tr3600-v1.dts b/target/linux/mediatek/dts/mt7987b-cudy-tr3600-v1.dts
--- a/target/linux/mediatek/dts/mt7987b-cudy-tr3600-v1.dts	2026-10-15 08:24:41.000000000 +0800
+++ b/target/linux/mediatek/dts/mt7987b-cudy-tr3600-v1.dts	2026-10-15 08:24:41.000000000 +0800
@@ -82,7 +82,16 @@
 
 &fan {
 	fan-supply = <&fan_3p3v>;
-	pwms = <&pwm 1 50000 0>;
+	/*
+	 * The fan is wired to PWM0 (PIN13), not PWM1.  pwm1_0 is PWM1@PIN7,
+	 * a pin shared with JTAG, so with the upstream binding the pwm-fan
+	 * driver still binds and a cooling device still appears - but PWM0 is
+	 * never claimed, stays at its idle level and the fan runs at full
+	 * speed, uncontrolled.  pwm_pins (group "pwm0") is what this board
+	 * needs and mt7987.dtsi already defines it.
+	 */
+	pwms = <&pwm 0 50000 0>;
+	cooling-levels = <0 128 200 255>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
@@ -159,18 +168,9 @@
 	};
 };
 
-&pio {
-	pwm1_pins: pwm1-pins {
-		mux {
-			function = "pwm";
-			groups = "pwm1_0";
-		};
-	};
-};
-
 &pwm {
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
-	pinctrl-0 = <&pwm1_pins>;
+	pinctrl-0 = <&pwm_pins>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
@@ -277,3 +277,42 @@
 	pinctrl-0 = <&uart0_pins>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
+
+/*
+ * mt7987.dtsi points the 40 C trip at fan cooling level 1, so with the stock
+ * table the fan runs at 50 % from 40 C upwards - that is, permanently.  This
+ * board idles around 55-64 C with the fan stopped, so the whole ramp is moved
+ * up: silent at idle, audible only under real load.
+ *
+ *   < 68 C : off
+ *   68 C   : level 1, PWM 128 (50 %)
+ *   78 C   : level 2, PWM 200 (78 %)
+ *   88 C   : level 3, PWM 255 (100 %)
+ *
+ * Measured on the board with each level forced for 80 s: level 1 at 25 %
+ * (PWM 64) cannot cool at all (63.7 C vs 64.1 C with the fan off), which is
+ * why the upstream 50 % step is kept instead of a gentler first step.
+ *
+ * cooling-levels keeps four entries because the upstream cooling-maps and the
+ * LuCI fan app both assume levels 0..3.  The 88 C top step still leaves 29 C
+ * to the 117 C CPU-throttling trip and 37 C to the 125 C critical trip, so
+ * this is an idle-noise fix, not a cooling downgrade.
+ */
+&cpu_thermal {
+	trips {
+		cpu_trip_active_low: active-low {
+			temperature = <68000>;
+			hysteresis = <5000>;
+		};
+
+		cpu_trip_active_med: active-med {
+			temperature = <78000>;
+			hysteresis = <5000>;
+		};
+
+		cpu_trip_active_high: active-high {
+			temperature = <88000>;
+			hysteresis = <5000>;
+		};
+	};
+};
